<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Časlav Klonimirović</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Časlav of Serbia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Časlav Klonimirović was Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in the 10th century and the last ruler of the Vlastimirović dynasty. Returning from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in the late 920s, he restored the Serbian principality with Byzantine support after the Bulgarian conquest, ruling over much of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. After his death, around the middle of the century, the state fragmented.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Časlav belonged to an exiled branch of the Serbian dynasty of the Vlastimirović and was brought up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> as a political hostage. His grandfather Strojimir and Strojimir's brother Gojnik had been expelled to Bulgaria by their brother Mutimir. His father Klonimir, born about 850, married a Bulgarian noblewoman, a match initiated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> khan Boris. Časlav was born before 889, probably at the Bulgarian capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliska">Pliska</a>, and his father tried to take the Serbian throne from Petar Gojniković but was killed in 897. The Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus</a> recorded the history of the Serbian principalities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a>, composed between 948 and 952.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 925 or 926 the Bulgarian ruler Simeon sent a large army against the Serbian ruler Zaharija, who fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>. Časlav was with it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> called the Serbian župani, or counts, to accept him as ruler, then captured them and Časlav and imprisoned them in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Serbia was devastated and came under nominal Bulgarian rule.</p><p>Simeon died on 27 May 927, and Časlav escaped with four followers and took the Serbian throne without the Bulgarian ruler's consent. The Byzantine emperor Romanos Lekapenos helped him and moved Serbian refugees to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Časlav swore loyalty to Romanos and was recognised as archon of Serbia, taking precedence among the Serbian principalities. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a>, he organised and settled the country. His accession is most recently dated to 927 or 928.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> raided Byzantine territory in 933 and 943, and Časlav may have defended the lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> as a Byzantine ally. His death is dated to 943 or 944, or to an unknown time, as the Byzantine sources that mention him were recorded before Romanos was ousted in December 944.</p><p>A later account based on folklore, the Chronicle of the Priest of Duklja, says that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyar</a> chieftain Kys raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and was killed by Časlav's men, and that Časlav was then captured in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a> and thrown bound into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a> river. The chronicle is considered unreliable for the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, though its personal names tend to be true. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> as a political entity disappears from Byzantine sources until the mid-eleventh century, and it seems to have broken up through infighting among the nobility.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Časlav” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prince <b>Časlav</b> restored it with Byzantine help from the late 920s, but after his death in the mid-10th century the state fragmented and Byzantium took Ras around 970.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-serbia-early-medieval">Principality of Serbia (early medieval)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preslav">Veliki Preslav</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.16,26.81&amp;year=964" aria-label="Veliki Preslav on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5197 632 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Časlav Klonimirović"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5647 902h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5647 902h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.16,26.81&amp;year=964">What was here in 964 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
